Scott's Theorem 7.4 says that each iterator \(\mathbf{Z}_n\) satisfies \(\mathbf{Z}_n : (A \to A) \to (A \to A)\) for every equivalence relation \(A\), and that any \(z \in \mathbf{P}\omega\) with this functionality for every such \(A\) and with \(z(f) = z(\lambda x.\, f(x))\) for every \(f\) equals \(\mathbf{Z}_n\) for some \(n\). `theorem_7_4` is that conjunction for restricted equivalences, the symmetric transitive relations of this section. `theorem_7_4_i` places \(\mathbf{Z}_n\) in the function space because the combinator and each application are graphs and the iteration relation preserves a related pair. `theorem_7_4_plotkin` is the step in which the iterate count still depends on \(f\) and \(x\): extensionality replaces \(f\) by the graph of its application, that graph advances the iterate equivalence, Theorem 7.1 puts \(z\) of the graph and then its value at the zeroth iterate into the equivalence, and the graph law rewrites the value as \(f^n(x)\). `theorem_7_4_sigma_index` produces one \(n\) for every tagged successor \(\sigma_j\), by the zero test, equation (7.18), a third application to the union of two successor graphs rewritten by (7.19), and monotonicity of application together with injectivity of pairing. Its gloss calls the hypothesis lying on every endomorphism retract of an endomorphism retract; the hypothesis used is membership of \(z\) in \((A \to A) \to (A \to A)\) for every restricted equivalence. `theorem_7_4_finite` takes \(j\) one past the maximum of the finite codes \(p\) and \(q\), relates the graph of \(e_p\) to its union with \(\sigma_j\) in the Plotkin equivalence, writes the two values as iterates, erases the tag, and equates the tags because a tag inside the pure iterate is excluded or pairing reads the singleton, which is the note's comparison \(f^q(x)\, A\, f^n(x) \cup (j, n)\) for \(j\) larger than \(f \cup x\). `theorem_7_4_unique` extends the finite equation to every argument by Scott continuity of application and of \(\mathbf{Z}\), determined by agreement on finite elements, with Theorem 1.3 for continuity in the function argument and the empty equivalence plus Theorem 7.1 supplying the graph equation that the proviso \(z(f) = z(\lambda x.\, f(x))\) gives, and concludes \(z = \mathbf{Z}_n\). The gathering sentence of `theorem_7_4` says this \(z\) is a combinator. The note and `theorem_7_4_unique` quantify over an arbitrary element of \(\mathbf{P}\omega\). The questions about which pure \(\lambda\)-terms have functionality, including \(\lambda x.\, x(x)\), are left open and are not stated. **Theorem:** partial — the conjuncts are the iterator theorem for an arbitrary element of \(\mathbf{P}\omega\), and the gathering sentence narrows that element to a combinator. **Proof:** yes — the iterate equivalence, the common index through (7.18) and (7.19), the finite tagged comparison, and the continuity extension follow the note's argument.
